Chapter: OtaFetch
  cites Net chapter NetDriver
  cites Net chapter Lwm2mFirmware
  cites Net chapter UdpIO
  cites Encode chapter Coap
  cites Foreword chapter OtaUpdate
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `codex/test/apps/ota-lwm2m-loopback` drives the OTA download by calling
 `fw-feed-response` with responses it builds itself through `serve-block`.
 That proves the block walk and the staging writes against our own `Coap`
 codec, and it is the BrotliBeatsOpus shape: the server half and the client
 half share every assumption. This binary is the socket the flow did not
 have, so the responses come from something that is not us.

 RFC 7959 Block2 is what the exchange rides. `fw-next-request` asks for one
 block at `fw-szx` 6, which is 1024 bytes, the largest that RFC defines;
 the server answers 2.05 Content with a Block2 option whose More bit says
 whether another follows. A response carrying no Block2 option at all is a
 whole-resource answer and ends the download.

 THE SERVER MUST BE REACHABLE, and on this box that means either a real
 CoAP server at the gateway address or `codex-vm -portfwd udp:HOST:5683`.
 The guest's outbound UDP is NAT'd to any port, which the run of
 `tools/lwm2m-client.codex` on 2026-08-18 measured directly
 (`NAT UDP: flow guest:5902 -> 10.0.2.2:5683`).

Section: Configuration

  of-local-mac : List Integer = net-driver-mac
  of-local-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  of-gateway-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]

  of-local-port : Integer = 5903
  of-server-port : Integer = 5683

  of-poll-fuel : Integer = 400000

 One round is one block, so the cap is the largest image this will fetch:
 400 blocks at 1024 bytes is 400 KB.

  of-rounds : Integer = 400

  of-bank-base : Integer = 536875008
  of-stage-addr : Integer = 536879104

 The manifest arrives over LwM2M as a Write to /5/0/1 in a deployment. This
 binary carries one so the fetch can be pointed at a resource by hand; the
 size and hash are the server's claims about what it will serve, and Gate A
 re-hashes what actually landed rather than trusting either.

  of-uri : Text = "/fw/image.cdx"

 The manifest's version must be NEWER than the context's current version or
 `ota-check-preconditions` refuses it as "not newer than current version",
 the manifest is never stored, and every request this builds carries no
 Uri-Path at all. That is a 7-byte datagram a server answers 4.04 to, and
 the reading it produces (`written=0 result=4`) is indistinguishable from a
 server that is simply not there.

  of-size : Integer = 4096

 The digest is the SERVER'S claim about what it will serve and is computed
 outside Codex entirely -- `build/ota-fetch-test.ps1` generates the payload
 on the host and hashes it there, so Gate A re-hashing what landed is a
 comparison between two things that never met. An empty hash here is not a
 neutral placeholder: Gate A refuses it and reports `result=6`
 (`fw-result-bad-package`) after a download that in fact succeeded.

  of-hash : List Integer = [15, 104, 58, 133, 235, 184, 18, 109, 127, 28, 174, 119, 74, 221, 135, 44, 34, 225, 12, 126, 34, 221, 165, 244, 76, 213, 149, 181, 215, 95, 125, 114]

  of-manifest : OtaManifest
  of-manifest = OtaManifest {
   firmware-uri = of-uri,
   firmware-size = of-size,
   firmware-hash = of-hash,
   version = ota-version-new 7 1000,
   min-battery = 0,
   requires-reboot = True
  }

  of-boot : BootConfig
  of-boot = boot-config-new (ota-version-new 1 500)

Section: The Download

  of-send-all : UdpSocket, List (List Integer), Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  of-send-all (sk) (outs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length outs then 0
   else act
    sent <- udp-io-send sk (list-at outs i)
    rest <- of-send-all sk outs (i + 1)
    sent + rest
   end

 A result other than the initial one is a verdict the flow has already
 reached, so the loop stops on it rather than asking for another block: a
 client that keeps requesting after `fw-fail` would bury the reason under
 whatever came next.

  of-finished : Lwm2mFw -> Boolean
  of-finished (c) =
   if fw-result-resource c /= fw-result-initial then True
   else fw-state-resource c == lwm2m-fw-state-value FwDownloaded

  of-run : UdpSocket, Lwm2mFw, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write, Flash, Device.Mmio] Lwm2mFw
  of-run (sk) (c) (msg-id) (rounds) =
   if rounds <= 0 then c
   else if of-finished c then c
   else act
    sent <- of-send-all sk [fw-next-request c msg-id []] 0
    r <- udp-io-recv sk of-poll-fuel
    if r.ur-got == False then c
    else act
     c2 <- fw-feed-response c (r.ur-data)
     of-run sk c2 (bit-and (msg-id + 1) 65535) (rounds - 1)
    end
   end

 A silent server ends the run rather than spending the whole round budget
 on a socket nobody is answering: the reading wanted is how many blocks
 landed before it went quiet, and that is already in `written`.

Section: Reporting

 `written` is the discriminating cell. It counts bytes that reached the
 staging address through `flash-write-page`, so it cannot be non-zero
 unless a response parsed as 2.05 Content, carried a block in the expected
 order, and was staged. `result` names the refusal when there was one.

  of-report : Lwm2mFw -> [Console.Write] Nothing
  of-report (c) = act
   print-line-uni ("ota-fetch"
     & " written=" & show (c.fw-written)
     & " next-block=" & show (c.fw-next-block)
     & " state=" & show (fw-state-resource c)
     & " result=" & show (fw-result-resource c)
     & " faults=" & show (c.fw-faults))
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Network.Read, Network.Write, Flash, Device.Mmio, Console.Write] Nothing = act
   let sk = udp-io-open of-local-mac of-local-ip of-local-port of-gateway-ip of-server-port
   in let c0 = fw-write-package-uri (fw-new of-boot (ota-version-new 1 500) of-bank-base of-stage-addr) of-manifest
   in act
    done <- of-run sk c0 1 of-rounds
    of-report done
   end
  end

